// A NAME IS BOUND ONCE PER BLOCK. `n = 42` then `n = 3.5` in one block
// was neither a binding nor a write, and nobody type-checked it: sema
// called the second one a fresh binding, because `lookup` walks from the
// top down and shadowing "falls out for free" (`sema_check.zen`), while
// the backend called it a store — `gen_c_stmt.is_store` answers yes to a
// plain `=` on a name already bound in the body. Two answers to one
// question, so the emitted C was `int64_t zu_l1n; zu_l1n = 42;
// zu_l1n = 3.5;` and the program printed 3 at exit 0. Zen has no cast
// keyword; an implicit float-to-int is exactly the wrong answer that
// looks plausible.
//
// The fix the message names is the DECLARATION's, not the write's: the
// two statements read identically, so only the first can say whether the
// name may be written again. `::=` says it.
//
// SHADOWING IS NOT WHAT IS REFUSED. A nested block still shadows and a
// match binder still shadows — `lookup_here` asks about THIS block, and
// `corpus/codegen/inlined_callee_keeps_its_own_local` is the case where
// two frames legitimately bind one name.
//
// ONE MISTAKE, ONE DIAGNOSTIC: the refused statement types as poison, so
// there is no second complaint about `3.5` not being an integer.
//
// positions: the second binding, then BOTH — a diagnostic that reports a
// collision and shows one side of it sends the reader hunting.

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, IoError> {
    n = 42;
    println("n starts as {}", n);
    n = 3.5;
    println("n is now {}", n);
    Ok(0);
}
